cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite the article listed under preferred-citation, and the OpenDengue database."
title: "Code and data for: Leakage-Controlled Tree-Ensemble Forecasting of Dengue Incidence Under Distribution Shift"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Glay
    given-names: Burgess Awalayah
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-2153-2695"
    affiliation: "Nanjing University of Posts and Telecommunications"
version: "1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-19"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Burgess-GLAY/dengue-leakage-forecasting"
abstract: >-
  Code, the harmonised Brazil state-week dengue panel (26 states, 2014-2024,
  derived from OpenDengue V1.3) and all result tables for a leakage-controlled
  forecasting study of weekly dengue incidence. Persistence, seasonal-naive,
  ARIMA(2,1,1) and ridge-autoregression baselines are compared with Random
  Forest and XGBoost at 1, 2 and 4 weeks ahead under three validation regimes
  (rolling-origin, a declared 2022-2024 distribution shift, and unseen states),
  together with an outbreak-alert classifier and grouped SHAP and permutation
  attribution. Includes the supplementary analyses requested in peer review.
keywords:
  - dengue
  - time-series forecasting
  - data leakage
  - distribution shift
  - gradient boosting
  - random forest
  - XGBoost
  - SHAP
  - epidemiological surveillance
  - Brazil
